"## F1.16 Ritual and belief as drivers of making\n\nBelief drives making through who commissions, what a rite requires, how often it repeats, where pilgrims spend and who reorganises the workshop; this unit follows those drivers, not theology.\n\n**Spec**\n\n| Row | Content |\n|---|---|\n| Essential question | When a rite or a belief asks for an object, what changes in who pays, who makes, how many, and by what rule? |\n| Opening move | **Object first.** A small wooden pagoda, unlabelled, with a paper roll inside (Met 30.47a–c, c. 764–770). Prompt: \"Someone ordered a million of these. Why?\" **Claim:** belief drove the earliest known printing at scale; each pagoda held a printed Buddhist invocation. **Counter-claim:** rule drove it; Empress Shōtoku ordered them after her court suppressed a rebellion in 764 (Met). The reveal: both. Belief and rule share one commission. Case: the Hyakumantō (One Million Pagodas). |\n",
"| Lab or main interactive | **Driver overlay** inside each object story (form 3). The learner tags evidence from the record to six drivers: commission, rule, material, repetition, economy (merit, pilgrimage, sale), regime (who reorganised the workshop). Tags are checked against the record text, not against opinion. On the mission case the overlay opens a **two-readings panel**: imposition and local reinvention, each with its source. |\n| Concept | 650 words. Six drivers, one example each: commission and merit (Wang Jie, 868); rule (keros in pairs); material (Raku bowls); repetition (a million pagodas; 80,000 Tripitaka blocks); economy (Canterbury badges; Hōryūji's 1908 appeal); regime (missions in Kongo, New Spain, Cuzco, Guaraní lands, Japan). Then the reverse: belief also destroys making (drives against keros; Canterbury, 1538). Then foodways: rites of drink and food commission vessels. Rule: we describe how rites shaped making; we do not explain or rank beliefs. |\n",
"| Cases | **1. The scroll made for two parents (F1.8; Mahayana Buddhism).** The Diamond Sutra from the Mogao library cave, dated 868, the earliest dated printed book known. Its colophon names the commissioner, Wang Jie, acting \"on behalf of his two parents\". The block cutter is unnamed. Coda: the Tripitaka Koreana, carved 1237–1248 against the Mongol invasions. **2. The mission as a making regime: a Kongo crucifix (F1.6; Latin Catholic Christianity and Kongo religion).** Met 1999.295.7, cast brass, 16th–17th century, made by a Kongo caster. Two readings, both sourced: the Portuguese demanded destruction of Kongo objects they called \"idols\"; Kongo artists took the crucifix \"far beyond any European precedent\". Strip of four comparisons (link out): Guaraní mission workshops, the 1539 feather *Mass of St Gregory*, the Cuzco painters' 1688 legal case, lacquer church furniture made in Japan for missionaries. **3. Two cups for one toast: a kero (F1.7; Andean religion).** Cleveland 1946.233, wood with inlaid pigments, 1550–1650. Made in pairs for exchanging chicha; targeted by Spanish campaigns; still made into the 1800s. Foodways strip: Raku tea bowl (Met 17.118.74), jebena and qvevri (li",
"nk out). |\n| Apply — maker | **The ritual your world serves.** Design one object for a rite in your own world: one you belong to, one whose holders have invited you, or a civic or everyday rite (a naming, a graduation, a shop opening, a shared meal). Answer the six drivers: who commissions, what rule the rite sets, what material it asks for, how many and how often, what economy it moves, and what happens to the object afterwards. Output: one drawing and a 150-word object story in the form-3 template. Never design for another community's sacred rite, and never copy a sacred design. |\n| Apply — briefer | **What the brief must know about the rite and its holders.** 200 words for a maker you would hire. Name the holders of the rite and who decides for them. Split the brief into three lists: fixed by the rite, open to the maker, and not ours to decide. Say how the holders will be asked, credited and paid. Mark each fact \"belong\", \"told by [holder]\", \"read in [source]\" or \"assumed\". |\n",
"| Partner | **Historian.** Question: \"Who paid, who made, what rule did they work to, and how do you know?\" The Historian cites records and never states what a community permits. Asked \"can I show this?\", it answers with what the record says about how the object was made and used, and sends the question to the holders. **Test it ends with:** every sentence in the learner's rite brief carries a source tag; untagged sentences are returned marked \"assumed\". |\n| Forms | 3 (object story: opener plus three cases), 8 (audio). |\n| Consumption forms | **Five-minute:** the pagoda object story as audio, with transcript. **Session (about 50 min):** opener, concept, three object stories with driver overlay, one Apply lane. **Reference:** claims sheet, driver table for all objects named, nodes list, protocol screen. |\n",
"| Media | Four object stories (form 3), each with image, driver overlay and audio description. Audio (form 8): \"The colophon\", about 4 minutes, on what Wang Jie's 868 dedication tells us about commission, merit and free copies. Every object story also ships as audio with a full transcript. No recitation of sacred texts: the pagoda's printed invocation is described, not read aloud. |\n| Artifact fields | **GRM.n:** the object's place in its rite (sequence, who handles it, what happens after). **SRC:** holders consulted and how each fact was learned. |\n| Threads | Attribution and Authority (commissioner versus maker credit; who speaks for a rite). Writing and Argument (two readings of the mission, each with evidence). Accessibility level 1 (transcripts, audio description of every object, alt text). Planetary Weight, light touch (materials a rite requires: clay, resin, tin-lead). |\n| Closing contribution | SRC line: \"Rite: [name]. Held by: [holders]. What I know and how: [belong / told by / read in]. What I left out, and why.\" |\n| Claims sheet | 20 claims below: 14 documented, 2 probable, 3 contested (both readings given), 1 interpretive (the unit's framing). Every URL was opened. |\n\n",
"**1. Responsibility block**\n\nThis unit must reach, with a case or an example:\n\n- **Belief traditions (five, from canon_beliefs.csv):** BEL022 Mahayana Buddhism (R1), BEL011 Latin Catholic Christianity (R2), BEL045 Kongo religion and minkisi (R1), BEL038 Andean religion (huacas) (R2), BEL024 Chan and Zen Buddhism (R2). BEL038 and BEL045 are not \"world religions\".\n- **World chapters (five of nine):** F1.8 East Asia (pagodas, Diamond Sutra, Tripitaka, Raku, Namban lacquer); F1.6 Africa (Kongo crucifix, jebena); F1.7 Americas (kero, featherwork, Cuzco, Guaraní missions); F1.11 Europe and the Mediterranean (Canterbury pilgrim badge); F1.9a West Asia before Islam (qvevri, Georgia, WA-CAU).\n- **Object types:** GAP054 Kongo crucifix, OBT075 kero, OBT082 tea bowl, OBT087 coffee pot (jebena), OBT069 qvevri; proposed pilgrim badge.\n- **Techniques and firsts:** TEC189 woodblock printing, INV039 woodblock printing (dated), TEC109 featherwork, TEC045 qvevri making.\n- **Sub-regions:** AS-CHN, AS-JPN, AS-KOR, AF-CEN, AF-HRN, AM-AND, AM-MES, AM-LAT, WA-CAU, EU-BLC.\n",
"- **Periods:** 8th–9th century (Nara, Tang); 13th century (Goryeo); 14th–17th century (pilgrimage, missions); living practice (Raku family, qvevri, jebena).\n\n**The Atlas carries instead:** ancient temple economies (BEL001–BEL003); Chola bronzes and Swamimalai casting (STY120, PRA109); thangka (TEC211); the kiswa (GAP053, PLC296); Jewish ritual silver (OBT202); icons and iconoclasm (OBT187, OCC026, OCC068); Ise (BEL032); Yorùbá and Haitian Vodou making (BEL042, BEL047); Aboriginal art centres, community-published only (MKR080, MKR084); ex-votos and amulets (OBT188, OBT189).\n\n**2. Protocol screen**\n\n**Excluded, and why:**\n\n- **Kongo minkisi, including nkisi nkondi (BEL045):** they hold empowering substances; canon flags looting. The crucifix carries the Kongo case.\n- **Andean ancestor bundles (mallqui) and huaca objects seized in the extirpation (OCC075):** human remains and sacred objects. Named in text, never shown.\n- **Hopi katsina masks and figures (BEL039):** canon records the masks as not for sale or display; figures left out too.\n",
"- **Aboriginal ceremonial objects and restricted designs (BEL050), Sámi drums (BEL053), Ghost Dance garments taken at Wounded Knee (BEL040):** sacred, secret or taken from the dead.\n- **Relic-bearing objects** (byeri guardians GAP057, Tibetan bone implements, relic deposits): human remains.\n- **Malagan figures (BEL055):** made to be displayed once, then left.\n- **Ethiopian altar tablets (tabot):** sacred; the holders' basis is not verified here.\n- **Recitation of sacred texts:** the pagoda's invocation is described, never read aloud.\n\n**Shown, with display basis and content note:**\n\n- **Pagoda (Met 30.47a–c):** made in a million copies for temples; Hōryūji gave some to donors in 1908. Content note: devotional object.\n- **Diamond Sutra:** made \"for universal free distribution\". Linked out. Content note: removed from Mogao in 1907.\n- **Kongo crucifixes (Met 1999.295.7; Cleveland 2010.444):** the Met records public use as pendants and insignia of office. Content note: conversion under Portuguese pressure, destruction of Kongo objects, and the Atlantic slave trade through the same kingdom. Enslaved people are named as people, never as goods.\n",
"- **Kero (Cleveland 1946.233):** a drinking vessel. Notice: Andean Indigenous material with living descendant communities. Content note: Spanish drives against Andean rites. One of Cleveland's two keros shows heaped body parts; show only the geometric one.\n- **Raku bowl (Met 17.118.74), pilgrim badge (Met 2001.310):** made for use and sale. Badge content note: the shrine was destroyed in 1538.\n- **Link-outs with Notice:** the feather *Mass of St Gregory* (Nahua makers); Guaraní mission sites.\n\n**3. Claims**\n\n| # | Claim | Confidence | Source opened | Depth |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | The unit's framing: belief drives making through commission, rule, material, repetition, economy and regime, and can be taught without judging any belief. | Interpretive | Our own argument | n/a |\n| 2 | The Diamond Sutra is dated 11 May 868, the earliest dated printed book known. Its colophon reads \"Reverently made for universal free distribution by Wang Jie on behalf of his two parents\". | Documented | https://idp.bl.uk/blog/the-diamond-sutra-on-display-text-panel-6-with-colophon/ ; https://www.cabinet.ox.ac.uk/worlds-earliest-dated-printed-book-diamond-sutra-868-ce | Full text (short pages) |\n",
"| 3 | Aurel Stein removed the Diamond Sutra with thousands of documents from the sealed Mogao library cave. Smithsonian says he \"bribed the abbot\"; canon OCC178 says he \"bought\". Give both wordings, attributed. | Contested (wording) | https://www.smithsonianmag.com/smart-news/Five-things-to-know-about-diamond-sutra-worlds-oldest-dated-printed-book-180959052/ | Summary article |\n| 4 | Empress Shōtoku (718–770) commissioned the One Million Pagodas to give thanks for help in suppressing the Emi rebellion of 764, and had them sent to Japan's major temples. | Documented | https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/44955 | Museum label |\n| 5 | The pagoda invocations were probably printed from bronze plates; some scholars say woodblocks. | Contested | Same Met record | Museum label |\n| 6 | In 1908 Hōryūji gave away 3,000 of its pagodas to contributors to a fund appeal. | Documented | Same Met record | Museum label |\n| 7 | The Tripitaka Koreana, about 80,000 woodblocks, was carved 1237–1248 \"in an appeal to the authority of the Buddha\" against the Mongol invasions. | Documented | https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/737/ | Summary (UNESCO listing) |\n",
"| 8 | The Kongo ruler was baptised in 1491; in the early 1500s the Kongo king made Catholicism the state religion and sponsored Christian art. | Documented | https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/318323 ; https://news.yale.edu/2019/03/05/art-historian-cecile-fromont-uncovers-kongos-christian-visual-culture | Label; news summary |\n| 9 | After the conversion, \"the Portuguese demanded destruction of traditional Kongo religious objects, which they labeled as 'idols.'\" | Documented | https://www.metmuseum.org/exhibitions/listings/2015/kongo/blog/posts/kongo-christian-art (Pereira and Windmuller-Luna, 2015) | Full text (essay) |\n| 10 | Inquisition records show locally made crucifixes traded illegally in Angola in the 1590s. Kongo artists varied figures \"far beyond any European precedent\". | Documented | Same Met essay | Full text (essay) |\n",
"| 11 | Two readings of the Kongo crucifix. Imposition: a Christian form arrived with Portuguese pressure and the destruction of Kongo objects. Local reinvention: Fromont argues missionaries \"landed in a place that had already converted\", and Kongo elites shaped Kongo Christianity. | Contested (both readings) | Met essay (claim 9); Yale News, 2019 | Full text; news summary |\n| 12 | Keros were made in pairs for exchanging chicha. They were \"frequent targets of early Spanish drives to destroy 'pagan' rites\" yet were made and used into the 1800s. Pigmented resin inlay was common before 1570. | Documented | https://www.metmuseum.org/toah/hd/kero/hd_kero.htm (Hecht, 2003); https://clevelandart.org/art/1946.233 | Full text (essay); label |\n| 13 | The Guaraní missions (reducciones) were settlements founded in the 1600s and 1700s on Guaraní lands, with workshops, worked mainly by Guaraní people, until the Jesuits' expulsion (1767–1768). | Documented | https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/275/ | Summary |\n",
"| 14 | The feather *Mass of St Gregory* (1539) was commissioned by Diego de Alvarado Huanitzin for Pope Paul III and made by an Indigenous artist. The Franciscan Pedro de Gante \"likely supervised\" it. | Documented (commission); probable (supervision) | https://smarthistory.org/featherworks/ (Kilroy-Ewbank) | Full text (essay) |\n| 15 | In 1688 Indigenous painters in Cuzco began a legal case that changed painting there; Cuzco devotional paintings were exported to cities in present-day Chile and Argentina. | Documented | https://smarthistory.org/our-lady-of-cocharcas-cuzco-school/ (Cohen-Aponte) | Full text (essay) |\n| 16 | Catholic missionaries in Japan commissioned lacquer church furnishings, including a folding lectern with the IHS emblem. | Documented | https://www.kyohaku.go.jp/old/eng/theme/floor1_6/past/laq_20141126.html | Summary (exhibition page) |\n| 17 | Chōjirō's tea-bowl making \"was stimulated and supervised by Rikyū\"; the Met attribution of 17.118.74 to Chōjirō is tentative. | Probable | https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/62898 | Museum label |\n",
"| 18 | Qvevri wine-making was inscribed by UNESCO in 2013; wine is used in \"secular and religious events and rituals\", and the wine cellar is called the holiest place in the home. | Documented | https://ich.unesco.org/en/RL/ancient-georgian-traditional-qvevri-wine-making-method-00870 | Summary (nomination text) |\n| 19 | A jebena is made of clay, \"usually crafted by an elderly woman\"; coffee is served in three rounds, the third called baraka, \"to be blessed\". | Documented (single source) | https://www.maadigitallab.org/blog/2023/08/22/at-the-heart-of-the-jebena-bunna-the-ethiopian-coffee-ceremony/ (Gupta, MAA Cambridge) | Blog |\n| 20 | Cast tin-lead badges showing St Thomas Becket's shrine were sold to pilgrims at Canterbury; the shrine was destroyed in 1538. | Documented | https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/473470 | Museum label |\n\n**4. Nodes**\n\n- **Opener (pagoda):** BEL022, TEC189, INV039, PRD120, POL179. Claims 4–6.\n- **Case 1:** PLC188 Mogao Caves, OCC178, PER181 Aurel Stein, INV039, TEC189, POL176 Goryeo, PLC199. Claims 2, 3, 7.\n",
"- **Case 2:** GAP054, POL020, CIV013, COM045 Bakongo, PLC054, BEL011, BEL045; strip: MKR027, SCH004, MKR019, TEC109, PER075, GAP005. Claims 8–11, 13–16.\n- **Case 3:** OBT075, BEL038, POL061, STY066, OCC075; foodways strip: OBT082, STY150, PER197, PER198, MKR041, BEL024, OBT087, BEL009 (context only), OBT069, PRA149, TEC045, COM228. Claims 12, 17–19.\n- **Concept (economy):** OCC059 for the shrine's end. Claim 20.\n\n**Proposed new canon rows:** Hyakumantō Darani (object); Empress Shōtoku (Kōken) (person); Hōryūji (place); Wang Jie (person, commissioner); Tripitaka Koreana (object); Emi rebellion, 764 (event); Afonso I of Kongo (person); Kongo Christianity (belief-tradition); Society of Jesus (institution); Pedro de Gante (person); pilgrim badge (object-type); shrine of St Thomas Becket, Canterbury (place); nanban lectern (shokendai) (object-type); jebena buna, Ethiopian coffee ceremony (practice).\n\n**5. Candidate open objects**\n\n| Object | Holder, accession | Date | Licence as stated | 1970 status | Record |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n",
"| One of the One Million Pagodas and invocation | The Met, 30.47a–c (objectID 44955) | ca. 764–70 | Public domain (`isPublicDomain: true`) | n/a (temple object; in the Met since 1930) | https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/44955 |\n| Crucifix, Kongo | The Met, 1999.295.7 (objectID 318323) | 16th–17th century | Public domain (`isPublicDomain: true`) | n/a (not archaeological); history before the 1999 gift not published | https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/318323 |\n| Crucifix, Kongo-style makers | Cleveland Museum of Art, 2010.444 | late 1800s–early 1900s (1880–1920) | CC0 | n/a; ownership chain Ralet, then Vanderstraete by 1945, Delenne 1963–2010 | https://clevelandart.org/art/2010.444 |\n| Kero (waisted cup), colonial Inka style | Cleveland Museum of Art, 1946.233 | after 1550 (1550–1650) | CC0 | Pass: in the museum since 1946; earlier history not recorded | https://clevelandart.org/art/1946.233 |\n| Black Raku tea bowl | The Met, 17.118.74 (objectID 62898) | early 17th century | Public domain (`isPublicDomain: true`) | n/a | https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/62898 |\n",
"| Pilgrim's badge of the shrine of St Thomas Becket | The Met, 2001.310 (objectID 473470) | 1350–1400 | Public domain (`isPublicDomain: true`) | **Not documented.** Gift of 2001, no find-spot published. Hold until cleared; otherwise use a drawing of our own. | https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/473470 |\n\nNo open record found for the Diamond Sutra, the Tripitaka, the jebena (MAA 2006.759 C), the *Mass of St Gregory* or a Namban lectern. Link out to each.\n\n**6. People and credit**\n\n- **Named commissioners:** Empress Shōtoku; Wang Jie; Diego de Alvarado Huanitzin; Sen no Rikyū. The Goryeo court for the Tripitaka.\n- **Named makers:** Chōjirō, tentatively (Met). Pedro de Gante as probable supervisor, not maker.\n- **Unnamed makers the unit must still name as makers:** the carvers, turners and printers of a million pagodas; the block cutter of 868; the Goryeo block carvers; the Kongo casters, \"a trained specialist in metallurgy\" (Fromont, Met); Andean kero carvers and inlayers; the Nahua feather artist; Guaraní carvers; the Japanese lacquerers; the jebena potter, \"usually an elderly woman\".\n",
"- **Left out by the credit:** the timber, clay and metal suppliers; Wang Yuanlu, the cave's keeper, often named only as \"the abbot\"; the donors of 1908; chicha brewers (not verified who, so not stated); the people held and sold through the Kongo kingdom in the same centuries.\n\n**7. Visual plan (zero cost)**\n\n- **Form 3, opener:** Met 44955 (public domain); our own cutaway drawing of where the roll sits.\n- **Form 3, case 1:** link out to the International Dunhuang Programme; our own diagrams of the scroll layout and of Haeinsa's block halls.\n- **Form 3, case 2:** Met 318323 (public domain) beside Cleveland 2010.444 (CC0), three centuries apart; comparison strip as link-outs.\n- **Form 3, case 3:** Cleveland 1946.233 (CC0) once confirmed geometric; Met 62898 (public domain); jebena and qvevri linked out; our own map of the chapters touched.\n- **Form 8:** our own narration, transcripts and audio descriptions.\n- No generated images of historical objects, people or places.\n\n**8. Success criteria**\n\n1. For one case, the learner names the commissioner, the maker (or \"unnamed maker\") and the rule the maker worked to, each tagged to a line in the record.\n",
"2. For the Kongo crucifix, the learner writes both readings in two sentences, each with its source, and does not merge them.\n3. The learner's rite brief (either lane) tags every fact as \"belong\", \"told by\", \"read in\" or \"assumed\", with no untagged sentence left.\n\n**9. Readings and risks**\n\n**Historian pre-read (pack v2) should check:** Pereira and Windmuller-Luna (Met, 2015) and Fromont, *The Art of Conversion* (2014), for the two readings; Hecht (2003) and Cummins, *Toasts with the Inca* (2002), on keros; IDP on the Diamond Sutra; Kilroy-Ewbank and Cohen-Aponte (Smarthistory).\n\n**Hold until a reader clears:** the Apply guardrail (own, invited or civic rites only); the Kongo content note and how the slave trade is named; the kero image and Andean Notice; the Stein wording; the audio script; any sentence that reads as the Historian stating what a community permits.\n\n**10. Fact-check list**\n\n- Canon rows used, R1: BEL022, BEL045, POL020, POL061, POL176, COM045, PLC188, INV039, TEC189, OBT082, OBT069, PRA149, STY150, BEL009. R2: BEL011, BEL024, BEL038, GAP054, OBT075, OBT087, SCH004, MKR019, OCC178, OCC059, PRD120, POL179, STY066, PLC054, COM228.\n",
"- INV039 note says a Korean dharani predates 751 but is undated: confirm before any \"earliest\" wording beyond \"earliest dated\".\n- Stein's year (1907) and the keeper's name, Wang Yuanlu: confirm at IDP (canon OCC178 has both; the Smithsonian page gives neither).\n- Which of Cleveland 1946.233 and 1957.143 is the geometric kero.\n- Met 1999.295.7 ownership before 1999; Met 2001.310 find-spot and pre-1970 history.\n- Guaraní missions: extension date and expulsion dates (UNESCO page gives 1983 inscription).\n- Namban lectern: record and holder (Kyoto exhibition page only).\n- Jebena: a second source; the ceremony's endonym (jebena buna) and its holders.\n- Endonyms: Bakongo; Kartvelebi; Guaraní; Nahua; Quechua- and Aymara-speaking communities.\n- UNESCO claims: Haeinsa (1995), qvevri (2013), Guaraní missions (1983).\n- Licences: re-check the four Met and two Cleveland records at publication.\n- Living people: none named; living practices described from published sources only.\n"
